<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solomon Linda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1909">1909</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Solomon Popoli Linda, Solomon Ntsele</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> Linda was a South African singer and composer from Natal whose song <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbube">Mbube</a>, improvised in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> recording studio in 1939 with his group the Evening Birds, reshaped the isicathamiya style of choral singing. A migrant worker in Johannesburg, he introduced a falsetto lead voice over several bass singers. Mbube reportedly sold more than 100,000 copies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, but Linda had sold his rights to Eric Gallo for ten shillings. The Weavers recorded it as Wimoweh in 1951 and the Tokens as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-lion-sleeps-tonight">The Lion Sleeps Tonight</a> in 1961, neither crediting him. He died poor in 1962; after his family sued Disney in 2004, a settlement gave them royalties and him a cowriting credit.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solomon-linda.jpg" alt="Solomon Linda" width="220" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solomon-linda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Solomon Linda</b> was born in rural Natal and, as a migrant worker and beer hall singer, founded the a cappella choir the Evening Birds after an earlier group of that name dissolved in 1933.</blockquote><a href="/songs/mbube">Mbube</a></li><li><blockquote>The song begins with &quot;Mbube&quot;, written by the South African singer <b>Solomon Linda</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-lion-sleeps-tonight">The Lion Sleeps Tonight</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in Soweto, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-29,24&amp;year=1943" aria-label="South Africa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5176 3224 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Solomon Linda"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5626 3494h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5626 3494h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-29,24&amp;year=1943">What was here in 1943? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
